---
import Study from "../../layouts/Study.astro";
import OzoneChart from "../../components/OzoneChart.astro";
import FoldExplainer from "../../components/FoldExplainer.astro";
---

<Study
  title="Shanghai Ozone ML"
  kind="Research"
  status="SURF-2026-0008 / Undergraduate research"
  repo="shanghai-ozone-ml"
  deck="Can a weather catalogue fitted on ozone-pollution days explain the wider year—and can a model predict a year it has never seen?"
>
  <OzoneChart />
  <div class="study-grid">
    <aside>
      <p>Context</p>
      <span>
        Shanghai
        <br />
        2021–2025
        <br />
        SOM · Random Forest · SHAP
      </span>
    </aside>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>A catalogue, with boundaries.</h2>
      <p>
        The study combines ERA5 synoptic fields, repeated Self-Organizing Maps,
        frozen-catalogue projection and leave-one-year-out Random Forest models.
        The matched analysis spans 1,826 Beijing-local days, including 142
        verified pollution days with MDA8 O₃ &gt; 160 μg m⁻³.
      </p>
      <p>
        K6 had higher mean label-invariant stability than K4 (ARI 0.636 versus
        0.573), but remained a provisional, medium-confidence catalogue. Only
        43.0% of all matched-day projections fell within the pollution-training
        95th-percentile distance threshold; in winter, the share was 0.9%.
      </p>
      <h2>Prediction outside the fitting year.</h2>
      <p>
        Five leave-one-year-out folds hold each year out of predictor-model
        fitting. The public model comparison reports the following out-of-fold
        results.
      </p>
      <FoldExplainer />
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>
            Random Forest model comparison · O₃ concentration errors in μg m⁻³
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Model</th>
              <th scope="col">R²</th>
              <th scope="col">RMSE</th>
              <th scope="col">MAE</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">M0</th>
              <td>0.685</td>
              <td>21.303</td>
              <td>16.192</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">M1</th>
              <td>0.738</td>
              <td>19.415</td>
              <td>14.933</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">M2</th>
              <td>0.811</td>
              <td>16.489</td>
              <td>12.747</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/KoreyoshiE/shanghai-ozone-ml/blob/main/results/RF_model_comparison.csv"
      >
        Inspect the model definitions and results ↗
      </a>
      <details>
        <summary>Methods and research role</summary>
        <div>
          <p>
            Four standardised ERA5 field blocks feed PCA; PCA95 retains 36
            components. K4 and K6 formal families each use 30 fixed seeds.
            Representative runs use the mean-ARI medoid rather than the most
            visually convenient run.
          </p>
          <p>
            After selection, PCA and SOM prototypes are frozen for projection.
            SHAP values are calculated for held-out predictions, using
            fold-specific training backgrounds.
          </p>
          <p>
            Han Che was the formally appointed undergraduate researcher and led
            the core data-science workflow. Hongxuan Li and Xinrui Li were SURF
            volunteers. Dr. Lei Han supervised the work through XJTLU and the
            Shanghai Academy of Environmental Sciences, as documented in the
            repository.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <div class="limitation">
        <h3>Association is not causation.</h3>
        <p>
          SHAP describes predictive attribution. Atmospheric radicals were not
          directly measured, and this work does not estimate radical
          concentrations or establish causal effects. Cool-season catalogue
          applicability is limited. Raw partner observations are restricted; a
          full upstream rerun requires authorised inputs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The public repository includes research code, selected outputs and a
        poster preview. No peer-reviewed publication is claimed here. Original
        figures and code are not republished in this portfolio because ownership
        and redistribution permission remain unconfirmed.
      </p>
      <a class="next-study" href="/research/wdcgg/">
        <small>Continue reading</small>
        <span>WDCGG Data Pipeline&nbsp;↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Study>
